<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Teutoburg Forest</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/9">9 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Teutoburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a> was fought in 9 CE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>, where Germanic tribes led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> ambushed and destroyed three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-legion">Roman legions</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Publius Quinctilius Varus</a>. It was one of the worst defeats in Roman military history. The disaster ended Roman efforts to conquer Germania east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, which became a lasting frontier of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-teutoburg-forest.jpg" alt="Battle of the Teutoburg Forest" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Agnete · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epitaph_des_Marcus_Caelius.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> conquered most of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a> and twice crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, but his engagements with Germanic tribes were inconclusive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> then neglected the continent for two decades, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> rebelled when troops left in 31 BCE. Publius Quinctilius Varus, appointed by Augustus to command in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a> in 7 CE, had been consul and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. The historian Michael McNally judges that he was sent for his political skill in handling the Germanic factions rather than for military ability.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> of the Cherusci had been taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as a hostage as a boy, received an aristocratic education, joined the Roman cavalry and commanded auxiliaries. He then returned home, still nominally loyal to Rome. His Roman training let him anticipate Roman tactics and deceive Varus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Varus planned a simple campaign for 9 CE: the army would cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and set up a summer camp in nominally pacified Cherusci territory. Sources say he took little military action that summer and held court instead, though the only contemporary account comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velleius-paterculus">Velleius Paterculus</a>, who had reason to dislike him. In late summer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> invented reports of a local rebellion. Others advised Varus that there was too little time to suppress it, but Arminius proposed a shorter route through the territory of the Angrivarii, and Varus took it.</p><p>The army set out on 8 September, strung out for 15 to 20 kilometres on forest tracks, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> meant to lead it toward Kalkriese, where the Angrivarii lay in ambush. In the late morning his Bructerii allies attacked along the line in a brief skirmish aimed at tiring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. A torrential downpour began, and Varus ordered a camp. The fighting went on between 8 and 11 September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> records that Augustus struck his head against the palace walls, shouting for Varus to give him back his legions. The numbers XVII, XVIII and XIX were never used for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-legion">Roman legions</a> again. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> took effective control, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a> in 14 CE. Arminius was poisoned by fellow Germans in 21 CE. Since the 15th century the battle has been seen as the event that ended Roman expansion at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and in the 19th century it became part of German nationalist mythology. Recent scholars point to other reasons for the frontier, such as supply from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> by river and the greater wealth of the Rhine lands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Teutoburg Forest” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Marcomanni left their mark on the name of Bohemia, through the Germanic Boiohaemum, and for a time their kingdom was the only part of Germania that Rome did not control before the <b>Battle of the Teutoburg Forest</b> in 9 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marcomanni">Marcomanni · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Augustus reduced the roughly 60 legions inherited from the civil wars to 28; after the loss of three in the <b>Teutoburg</b> disaster of 9 CE the total stood at 25, rising gradually to around 33 by the reign of Septimius Severus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
